              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p19-g-q4-a-task",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "How many grid points does the diagonal line pass through?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "10 grid points.",
          "worked": "The greatest common divisor of 36 and 45 is 9. The grid points divide the diagonal into 9 equal intervals, so there is one more point than intervals.\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\gcd(36,45)+1\\\\N&=9+1\\\\N&=10.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-g-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "How many squares does the diagonal line pass through?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "72 squares.",
          "worked": "There are $9-1=8$ interior grid points on the diagonal. A staircase count gives $36+45-1$ cells, but each interior grid-point crossing reduces this count by one.\n$$\\begin{align*}S&=36+45-1-8\\\\S&=72.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-g-q4-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p19-g-q4-general-stem-text",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A diagonal line is now drawn from corner to corner of an ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "m\\times n",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " square grid.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p19-g-q4-c-task",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "How many grid points does the diagonal line pass through?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\gcd(m,n)+1$ grid points.\n\nDivide both dimensions by their greatest common divisor to obtain the smallest grid-point step along the diagonal.",
          "worked": "Let $g=\\gcd(m,n)$, $m=gu$ and $n=gv$, where $u$ and $v$ have no common factor greater than 1. A grid point $(p,q)$ lies on the diagonal when its gradient from the origin is $\\frac{n}{m}$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{q}{p}&=\\frac{n}{m}\\\\\\frac{q}{p}&=\\frac{v}{u}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the non-origin grid points are $(u,v),(2u,2v),\\ldots,(gu,gv)$. There are $g$ such points. Including the origin gives\n$$N=g+1=\\gcd(m,n)+1.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-g-q4-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "How many squares does the diagonal line pass through?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$m+n-\\gcd(m,n)$ squares.\n\nSubtract one from the staircase count for each interior grid-point crossing.",
          "worked": "Let $g=\\gcd(m,n)$. There are $g-1$ interior grid points on the diagonal. The crossed cells form a staircase with an initial count of $m+n-1$. At each interior grid point, the diagonal enters the next cell directly, reducing that count by one.\n$$\\begin{align*}S&=m+n-1-(g-1)\\\\S&=m+n-g\\\\S&=m+n-\\gcd(m,n).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
  },
